∇Z←G8 X
 Z←'→L1' ⎕EA 'UNDEFINED_NAME_XYZ'
 Z←999
 →0
L1: Z←-1
∇
      G8 5
¯1

      )ERASE G8

      ⍝ Bugs28 #56 regression (Bugs27 #36 residual): ⎕EM after a
      ⍝ failing ⎕EA showed the correct error NAME (row 1, e.g. DOMAIN
      ⍝ ERROR) but 2 BLANK rows instead of the failed statement text
      ⍝ and its carets -- ⎕ET (which only needs the numeric code) was
      ⍝ already right. Root cause: the ⎕EA macro (Macro.def's
      ⍝ Z__A_Quad_EA_B) forwards only ⎕EC's numeric ET (major/minor)
      ⍝ to ⎕ES on the error path, never its RES (μ9) -- which
      ⍝ Quad_EC::eoc() ALSO populates, even on the error branch, with
      ⍝ the same 3-line ⎕EM-shaped message ⎕EM itself would show.
      ⍝ Prefix::execute_EA_fallback() then reconstructed a bare,
      ⍝ code-only Error from scratch, with no way to recover that
      ⍝ text. Fixed by forwarding μ9 as a 6th ⎕ES payload element and
      ⍝ having Prefix::handle_QUAD_ES_ERR() recover the failed-
      ⍝ statement text and caret positions from it.
      ⍝
      '⎕EM' ⎕EA '1÷0'
DOMAIN ERROR
      1÷0   
      ^^    

      ⍝ control: ⎕ET was already correct
      ⍝
      '⎕ET' ⎕EA '1÷0'
5 4

      ⍝ same check, but at top level (A is just '1', then ⎕EM/⎕ET are
      ⍝ queried in the same statement so the error is still current)
      ⍝
      '1' ⎕EA '1÷0' ⋄ ⎕EM ⋄ ⎕ET
1
DOMAIN ERROR
      1÷0   
      ^^    
5 4

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #7) -- CLOSED AS NOT A BUG
      ⍝ (2026-09-29, after discussion with the user): an SI-modifying
      ⍝ command as the A operand -- reached only when B fails, ⎕EA's
      ⍝ whole point -- reaches Command.cc and really executes, e.g.
      ⍝ ')OFF' ⎕EA '1÷0' really ends the session. An earlier session
      ⍝ "fixed" this by catching E_COMMAND_PUSHED in
      ⍝ Prefix::execute_EA_fallback() and turning it into a DOMAIN_ERROR
      ⍝ instead -- that was wrong and has been reverted. ⎕EC/⎕EA/⎕EB
      ⍝ exist to make EXECUTION ERRORS in the controlled operand (B)
      ⍝ reportable, not to veto what the program explicitly asks A to
      ⍝ do -- ')OFF' ⎕EA INIT ("run INIT; if it fails, don't continue
      ⍝ in a half-initialized workspace") is a legitimate, intentional
      ⍝ pattern, and A is documented to execute exactly as if directly
      ⍝ executed, unlike the sandboxed B. E_COMMAND_PUSHED is not a
      ⍝ hazard needing a guard here: it is the same, already-safe,
      ⍝ generic mechanism used for a plain execution of an SI-modifying
      ⍝ command at any call depth (Bif_F1_EXECUTE.cc throws it; only
      ⍝ Command.cc's main loop catches it, once the stack is already
      ⍝ fully and safely unwound) -- catching it one frame early here
      ⍝ just short-circuited that.
      ⍝
      ⍝ )OFF/)CLEAR/)SAVE themselves can't be exercised for real in this
      ⍝ shared-process test suite (they would end or corrupt the *whole*
      ⍝ test3 run, not just this file) -- verified live instead, outside
      ⍝ the harness: ')OFF' ⎕EA '1÷0' (B failing) cleanly exits the
      ⍝ session (no "still alive" line reached afterward). )SIC is on
      ⍝ the same guarded-command list (Bif_F1_EXECUTE.cc) and goes
      ⍝ through the identical E_COMMAND_PUSHED path, but only clears the
      ⍝ )SI stack -- safe to actually run here, and enough to prove the
      ⍝ command really reaches Command.cc instead of being turned into a
      ⍝ DOMAIN_ERROR. Also confirms the whole ⎕EA statement (including
      ⍝ any assignment around it) never completes when A turns out to be
      ⍝ a real command -- same as a plain execution of one would.
      ⍝
      ⍝ Blake's original report also claimed )SAVE from within ⎕EA
      ⍝ persists a phantom transient SI frame into the saved workspace
      ⍝ (Bugs30 #25 reappearing). Re-verified live (2026-10-02): a real
      ⍝ ')SAVE ...' ⎕EA '1÷0' followed by )CLEAR/)LOAD/)SI DOES show a
      ⍝ nameless "*" )SI entry -- but so does a PLAIN ⍎')SAVE ...' at
      ⍝ top level, with no ⎕EA/⎕EB involved at all (an earlier version
      ⍝ of this comment claimed the ⎕EA case came back clean; it no
      ⍝ longer does). The saved "*" is the invoking statement's own
      ⍝ )SI frame, so it belongs to Command.cc's/)SAVE's own frame
      ⍝ handling, not to this ticket; per the user, if it's ever
      ⍝ addressed it should be by having )SAVE ignore its own invoking
      ⍝ )SI frame, not by anything here.
      ⍝
      ⍝ )SIC is on the same guarded-command list (Bif_F1_EXECUTE.cc) as
      ⍝ )OFF/)CLEAR/)SAVE, goes through the identical E_COMMAND_PUSHED
      ⍝ path, but -- run here from an already-empty )SI -- has no
      ⍝ visible effect of its own either way, making it safe to run
      ⍝ for real inside this shared-process suite. Its only job here is
      ⍝ to prove the command reaches Command.cc silently (as a real,
      ⍝ successful )SIC would) instead of being turned into the old
      ⍝ "DOMAIN ERROR ... cannot be executed from within ⎕EC/⎕EA/⎕EB":
      ⍝ no output at all is the correct, passing result.
      ⍝
      ')SIC' ⎕EA '1÷0'
      )SI

      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

